"A data-flow diagram describes how named data moves between external entities, transformations, and stores.\n\nUse when: Explain system information movement independently of execution timing. Decompose a transformation while preserving its external inputs and outputs.\n\nAvoid when: Exact control order or infrastructure placement is needed.\n\nVisual grammar: Distinct symbols encode processes, stores, and external entities. Directed arrows name the data transferred using noun phrases. Numbered processes support balanced decomposition into deeper views.\n\nBuild: Choose one notation and a bounded decomposition level. List external inputs, outputs, and persistent stores. Add processes that transform or move data rather than mere control decisions. Place stores near their processes and route flows through open corridors with short labels.\n\nCheck: Every process has meaningful input and output. Child views preserve their parent’s external flow balance. Flows connect through processes rather than implying direct external-to-store access without explanation. Flow labels and process names remain separate from connectors.\n\nPitfalls: Using arrows to mean execution order. Mixing several decomposition levels into one unbounded map.\n\nExample: An Import process receives an Upload, writes Raw Records, and emits a Validation Report to the submitter.\n\nAlternatives: ETL pipeline diagram, Threat-model data-flow diagram."
